WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
This is the TSM Enterprise Command Center, where IT operations work starts. Everything you will see runs in the real application, against demo records.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.800
The copilot assistant is one click away from the command center.

3
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:27.700
A technician opens the L one Ticket Copilot. The problem it targets: ticket context is scattered across incidents, asset records and procedures, and every technician pieces it together differently.

4
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:36.500
An offboarding ticket arrives. A departing employee's laptop has to come back to inventory, and its device record has to be updated.

5
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:49.900
TSM reads the ticket text and classifies it as offboarding. It sets the next action, and it shows closure as blocked, because the required evidence isn't there yet. The technician knows what is missing before doing anything.

6
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.200
To verify the return, the technician enters the laptop's asset tag.

7
00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.700
TSM looks up the asset record. This environment uses demo records, and the screen says so.

8
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.100
The technician records the outcome: returned to stock.

9
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:12.400
And confirms it personally. A person attests to the physical return. The system does not assume it.

10
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.400
Asset recovery is verified.

11
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.400
Next, the asset record itself has to change. The technician moves to Asset Lifecycle.

12
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.200
The incident, the asset, the technician, and the reason for the return are filled in.

13
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:37.900
The assistant recommends Return to Inventory, at medium confidence, and labels it as draft assistance only. It recommends. It does not act.

14
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:46.400
Nothing changes until the technician authorizes it. That boundary is the point: the AI prepares, a person decides.

15
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.400
The action is confirmed, and the confirmation is recorded.

16
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.100
From here the technician can draft the resolution note from the evidence gathered so far.

17
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:04.700
Or, if the work needs Tier two, build an escalation package, so the next team doesn't start from scratch.

18
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.400
Back on the ticket, closure requires explicit evidence.

19
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.400
The user is verified,

20
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.400
the work is confirmed,

21
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.400
the fix is tested,

22
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.800
and the final work note is confirmed.

23
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:33.400
TSM re-evaluates, and closure is still blocked. The location hasn't been verified, so the ticket cannot close on incomplete evidence. The gate holds even when everything else looks done.

24
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:42.300
A different ticket: a software install request. TSM classifies it as a software task, and the required steps change with it.

25
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.700
The technician enters the request number.

26
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.100
TSM finds the request, again from demo records.

27
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.100
Outcome: fulfilled.

28
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.400
The technician confirms the software is installed,

29
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.500
and attests to the fulfillment.

30
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.500
Fulfillment is verified.

31
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.500
Leadership sees the same work from the L one executive view.

32
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:25.300
The same loop, every ticket: understand the ticket, prepare the action, verify it, a technician confirms, the result is recorded, and the work hands off cleanly. The aim is consistency: less avoidable re-investigation, and evidence you can audit.

